<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Syriam</h1><p class="st-meta">1599 CE – 1613 CE · capital Syriam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Portuguese adventurer's private fief at the port of Syriam in Lower Burma, which lasted fourteen years before the Burmese retook it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filipe-de-brito-e-nicote">Filipe de Brito e Nicote</a>, a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> in the service of the king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Mrauk U Kingdom</a>, was placed in charge of the port of Syriam, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> in 1599. He soon turned it into his own base, obtained backing from the Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and was recognised as its governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filipe-de-brito-e-nicote">De Brito</a> ruled with a small Portuguese garrison and local allies, collecting customs on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> trade and controlling access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a>. He built a fort and church, encouraged Catholic conversions and allied with local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> leaders. He was notorious among Burmese for looting pagodas to melt down their bells for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaukpetlun">Anaukpetlun</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom</a> besieged Syriam in 1613 and took it after a few months. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filipe-de-brito-e-nicote">De Brito</a> was executed by impalement, and the Portuguese captives were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> as royal gunners, where their descendants, the Bayingyi, still live.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1599 CE – 1613 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.52%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1599 CE – 1613 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Syriam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Filipe de Brito e Nicote</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Mon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Syriam, Thanlyin (Portuguese)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1606&amp;focus=portuguese-syriam" aria-label="Portuguese Syriam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7252 1373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Syriam lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7572 1629h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7572 1629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Myanmar. <a href="/atlas?year=1606&amp;focus=portuguese-syriam">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

